WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.691
A health maintenance organization does two things that insurers and hospitals normally do separately.

00:00:04.691 --> 00:00:08.042
It collects a fixed prepaid amount for each member, and

00:00:08.042 --> 00:00:11.393
it arranges or directly provides the care those members need.

00:00:11.393 --> 00:00:15.414
Because it holds the money and runs the care, it has every

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:19.435
incentive to keep members healthy and to manage how services are used.

00:00:19.435 --> 00:00:23.121
An HMO operates in a defined service area; members who travel

00:00:23.121 --> 00:00:26.807
outside it are generally covered only for emergencies and urgent care.

00:00:26.807 --> 00:00:30.158
In Maryland an HMO is authorized under the Health-General Article,

00:00:30.158 --> 00:00:33.509
holds a certificate of authority from the Insurance Commissioner and

00:00:33.509 --> 00:00:36.860
answers to the Department of Health on quality of care.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.213
When Priya’s employee joins the HMO she selects

00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:43.566
a primary care physician, often called the gatekeeper.

00:00:43.566 --> 00:00:47.338
The PCP delivers routine and preventive care and decides

00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:50.691
when a referral to a specialist is needed.

00:00:50.691 --> 00:00:54.882
Without the referral, specialty care is not covered except for

00:00:54.882 --> 00:00:59.073
services the plan designates as open access, such as obstetrics

00:00:59.073 --> 00:01:02.845
and gynecology or, under Maryland law, certain direct-access situations.

00:01:02.845 --> 00:01:08.713
The gatekeeper model controls costs by making sure specialists see patients who need them.

00:01:08.713 --> 00:01:12.066
It also means the choice of PCP is

00:01:12.066 --> 00:01:15.000
the most important decision a member makes.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.300
HMO members usually pay a flat copayment per visit or prescription rather than

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.192
a deductible and coinsurance, and the plan pays the provider the rest.

00:01:25.192 --> 00:01:29.676
Many HMOs pay their physicians by capitation: a fixed monthly amount

00:01:29.676 --> 00:01:34.161
per assigned member, whether that member visits once or ten times.

00:01:34.161 --> 00:01:38.238
That is the prepaid basis carried down to the provider.

00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:39.053
Models differ.

00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:43.537
A staff model employs its physicians; a group model contracts with

00:01:43.537 --> 00:01:48.022
one medical group; an IPA model contracts with independent physicians who

00:01:48.022 --> 00:01:52.506
also see other patients; a network model contracts with several groups.

00:01:52.506 --> 00:01:55.360
All share prepaid financing and limited choice.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.121
HMOs built their reputation on prevention.

00:01:58.121 --> 00:02:02.723
Annual physicals, immunizations, screenings and well-child visits are covered, often

00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:07.326
with no copayment, because preventing illness lowers the plan’s costs.

00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:11.467
Primary care is delivered by the PCP; referral care

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:15.149
is delivered by specialists on the PCP’s order.

00:02:15.149 --> 00:02:18.831
Hospital services, including inpatient admissions and surgery, are

00:02:18.831 --> 00:02:22.513
covered when authorized and delivered at participating hospitals.

00:02:22.513 --> 00:02:28.035
Other basic services include diagnostic laboratory and imaging, outpatient surgery, maternity care,

00:02:28.035 --> 00:02:33.097
mental health and substance use treatment at parity, and prescription drugs.

00:02:33.097 --> 00:02:38.620
Members are called subscribers or enrollees and hold an evidence of coverage.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.875
Emergency care is the exception to every network rule.

00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:46.939
If Priya’s employee is injured on vacation in Ohio, the HMO must cover emergency

00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:52.003
services without a referral and without regard to the service area, judged by the

00:02:52.003 --> 00:02:56.705
prudent layperson standard: would a reasonable person believe the symptoms needed immediate attention?

00:02:56.705 --> 00:03:01.046
Federal and Maryland law require coverage of emergency care at the in-network

00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:05.024
cost sharing level and protect the member from surprise balance bills.

00:03:05.024 --> 00:03:09.003
Once stabilized, the member is expected to return to the network

00:03:09.003 --> 00:03:12.620
for follow-up care, and the HMO may arrange a transfer.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.670
The exam describes a plan and asks you

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.721
to name it or to decide a claim.

00:03:18.721 --> 00:03:23.296
Look for the markers: prepaid premium, a primary care physician who must

00:03:23.296 --> 00:03:27.871
approve referrals, copayments rather than coinsurance, no coverage out of network except

00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:32.066
emergencies, a defined service area, members or subscribers rather than insureds.

00:03:32.066 --> 00:03:35.879
If a question says a member saw a specialist without

00:03:35.879 --> 00:03:39.310
a referral for a nonemergency, the HMO may deny.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.360
If it says the member had chest pain

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.029
in another state, the HMO must pay.

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:48.080
Remember Maryland’s regulatory split: certificate from the Insurance

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.130
Commissioner, quality oversight from the Department of Health.
